#
# What we vendor from Gadgetbridge, and what we deliberately leave behind.
#
# Read by pull-gadgetbridge.sh. Paths are relative to Gadgetbridge's
# app/src/main/java/nodomain/freeyourgadget/gadgetbridge/ and are copied into
# device-garmin/ under the SAME package names. That is deliberate: it makes a
# future pull a file copy rather than a rewrite, keeps upstream diffs
# applicable, and makes the provenance of every vendored file obvious from its
# package. Our shims for the pieces we do not take live in the same namespace,
# in :device-gb-compat.
#
# Adding another vendor later (Amazfit, Xiaomi, Polar) means adding its
# packages here and re-running the script. The shim layer is the reusable
# investment; the per-vendor cost should be close to a copy.
#
# Syntax:
#   include <path>        Java, from app/src/main/java/nodomain/freeyourgadget/gadgetbridge/
#   include-proto <path>  .proto, from app/src/main/proto/ (compiled at build time)
#   include-codegen <path> from FitCodeGenerator/src/main/, into :fit-codegen
#   exclude <path>        carve-out applied after includes
#   (blank lines and # comments ignored)

# ── FIT codec ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reading and writing FIT records. 53 of 55 files are free of Gadgetbridge's
# database; the two that are not are its importer, which we explicitly do not
# want — Tracks ships raw FIT bytes to the server and lets the server parse
# them, so duplicating that here would be a second parser to keep in step.
include service/devices/garmin/fit
exclude service/devices/garmin/fit/FitImporter.java
exclude service/devices/garmin/fit/FitAsyncProcessor.java

# ── FIT message code generator ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not app code — a build tool, and a mandatory one. Gadgetbridge checks in only
# four FIT message classes; the other ~400 are generated at build time from
# Garmin's profile JSON. Even those four are only halves: FitRecord extends a
# generated AbstractFitRecord. So without this, nothing in the FIT layer
# compiles at all.
#
# It lands in :fit-codegen, a plain JVM module, so the generator itself never
# reaches the APK — only its output does. Same package names as upstream, so a
# refresh stays a file copy. Paths are relative to FitCodeGenerator/src/main/.
include-codegen java/nodomain/freeyourgadget/gadgetbridge/service/devices/garmin/fit/codegen
include-codegen resources/fit_profile.json

# ── GFDI protocol ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The message layer: file transfer (which is how workouts and courses get onto
# a watch), notifications, music control, weather, device settings.
#
# messages/status/SetFileFlagsStatusMessage.java carries a small Tracks-local
# patch on top of upstream — search the file itself for "Tracks-local patch" —
# that publicizes the FlagsStatus enum and adds getters for it and the file
# identifier. Upstream parses this response and drops it; Tracks awaits it to
# know whether a course delete over BLE actually applied. A fresh pull
# overwrites it silently; reapply that hunk (a `git diff` against the previous
# vendored copy shows exactly it) before committing the refresh.
include service/devices/garmin/messages

# ── Protobuf definitions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Garmin's protobuf services — calendar sync, music control, device settings,
# authentication. These are .proto sources compiled by the protobuf Gradle
# plugin, not checked-in Java, so they land in a different source root.
include-proto garmin

# ── Vendor identification ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Capability flags a watch reports about itself, and the preference keys the
# protocol code reads. Not the coordinator or the sample providers — those are
# Gadgetbridge's device registry and its database, both of which Tracks has its
# own answer for.
include devices/garmin/GarminCapability.java
include devices/garmin/GarminPreferences.java

# ── BLE transport ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The transaction queue, GATT callbacks, and connection scaffolding every BLE
# device in Gadgetbridge sits on. Vendored whole because it is vendor-neutral:
# a future Amazfit or Polar implementation uses the same queue, so this is the
# part that pays for itself twice.
include service/btle
# Gadgetbridge's own scanning and multi-device plumbing. Tracks pairs one watch
# through CompanionDeviceManager, which hands back an association that survives
# reboots and lets the OS wake us on presence — a large battery saving over
# scanning, and the reason none of this is needed.
exclude service/btle/BLEScanService.java
exclude service/btle/BleIntentApi.java
exclude service/btle/AbstractBTLEMultiDeviceSupport.java
# Standard-GATT profiles for devices that are not watches — a PineTime
# filesystem, blood-pressure cuffs, weight scales, thermometers. Measured, not
# assumed: nothing outside service/btle/profiles/ references any of them. Only
# the abstract base (AbstractBleProfile, kept) and alertnotification's
# AlertCategory enum (used by BLETypeConversions) are actually reachable.
#
# They are also the most expensive files to keep: they drag in Gadgetbridge's
# UI strings, its zip/URI helpers, PineTime constants, and kotlin-parcelize —
# a whole Gradle plugin — for capabilities Tracks does not have.
exclude service/btle/profiles/adablefs
exclude service/btle/profiles/battery
exclude service/btle/profiles/bloodpressure
exclude service/btle/profiles/deviceinfo
exclude service/btle/profiles/healthThermometer
exclude service/btle/profiles/heartrate
exclude service/btle/profiles/weightScale
exclude service/btle/profiles/alertnotification/AlertNotificationProfile.java

# ── Garmin device support ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The communicator (GFDI over BLE, both protocol versions) and the handlers
# that drive it. GarminSupport itself is excluded — see below.
include service/devices/garmin/communicator
include service/devices/garmin/ChecksumCalculator.java
include service/devices/garmin/FileToDownload.java
# FileTransferHandler.java carries one Tracks-local patch on top of upstream —
# search the file itself for "Tracks-local patch": parseDirectoryEntries()
# hands a listing straight to addFileToDownloadList when the newer sync
# protocol is in use, rather than parsing entries the protobuf listing will
# supply anyway. A fresh pull overwrites it silently; reapply the hunk (a
# `git diff` against the previous vendored copy shows exactly it) before
# committing the refresh.
#
# It used to carry two more — DirectoryEntry.getFileNumber() and
# requestDirectoryListing()/DirectoryListingListener — which existed to find a
# course's current fileIndex by matching the byte size a push sent, so that a
# SetFileFlagsMessage DELETE could address it. That whole approach is gone:
# the watch answered DELETE with ERROR, course removal was rebuilt on
# Explore-sync tombstones, and the machinery sat unreachable until it was
# removed. Fewer hunks to reapply on the next refresh.
include service/devices/garmin/FileTransferHandler.java
include service/devices/garmin/FileType.java
include service/devices/garmin/FitLocalMessageHandler.java
include service/devices/garmin/GarminByteBufferReader.java
include service/devices/garmin/GarminPrefs.java
include service/devices/garmin/GarminTimeUtils.java
include service/devices/garmin/GarminUtils.java
include service/devices/garmin/MessageHandler.java
include service/devices/garmin/NotificationsHandler.java
include service/devices/garmin/ProtocolBufferHandler.java
include service/devices/garmin/agps
include service/devices/garmin/deviceevents
# Garmin's on-watch HTTP proxy — the watch asks the phone to fetch things for
# it. Left out of the first pass: it drags in Gadgetbridge's contacts entities
# and internet firewall, and nothing Tracks needs yet goes through it. Weather
# reaches the watch over GFDI instead.
#include service/devices/garmin/http

# ── Support types the protocol code refers to ────────────────────────────────
# Small, dependency-free value types. Vendored rather than reimplemented
# because they are part of the protocol's vocabulary — an ActivityKind or a
# NotificationType we invented would have to be mapped back to theirs at every
# call site, which is more code and more places to be wrong.
include deviceevents/GBDeviceEvent.java
include deviceevents/GBDeviceEventFindPhone.java
include deviceevents/GBDeviceEventMusicControl.java
include deviceevents/GBDeviceEventUpdatePreferences.java
include deviceevents/GBDeviceEventVersionInfo.java
include model/ActivityKind.java
include model/ActivityPoint.java
include model/GPSCoordinate.java
include model/NotificationType.java
include util/ArrayUtils.java
include util/CheckSums.java
include util/StringUtils.java
include util/LimitedQueue.java
include util/GBTarFile.java
include util/Prefs.java
include util/FileUtils.java
include util/GBToStringBuilder.java
# The newer protobuf file-sync protocol hands files over zlib-deflated, unlike
# the directory-listing path which streams them raw. Without this the V2
# download path has nothing to inflate with.
include util/CompressionUtils.kt
include util/protobuf
include util/gpx/model

# Notification, call, and canned-reply payloads. Pure value types with no
# coupling — the vocabulary the GFDI notification layer speaks, so inventing
# our own would mean translating at every call site.
include model/NotificationSpec.java
include model/CallSpec.java
include model/CannedMessagesSpec.java

# Two GDI protobuf sub-handlers that are genuinely self-contained. The others
# (AppConfig, ExploreSync, Http, DataTransfer) reach into Gadgetbridge's UI,
# database and exporters, so they are shimmed instead — see SHIMS.md.
include service/devices/garmin/FileSyncServiceHandler.kt
include service/devices/garmin/EcgServiceHandler.kt

# ── Left behind, on purpose ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   util/GB.java
#       707 lines of toasts, notification channels, and GBApplication access —
#       Gadgetbridge's UI layer wearing a utility class's name. :device-gb-compat
#       provides the handful of methods the protocol code actually calls,
#       routed to Tracks' own logging instead of a toast.
#
#   util/DateTimeUtils.java
#       Vendored, then measured and dropped. Only five of its methods are
#       reachable, none of them the two that need
#       com.github.pfichtner:durationformatter — a JitPack dependency. JitPack
#       builds from a git tag at request time and is not reproducible, which
#       matters because F-Droid is the eventual distribution target. Shimmed in
#       src/compat with the five methods, and no new repository.
#
#   util/AndroidUtils.java
#       Was vendored; measured and dropped. Exactly one of its thirteen public
#       methods is reachable from the code we keep — safeUnregisterBroadcastReceiver,
#       called twice from BondAction. The rest is theming, file sharing and
#       toasts, and it was the single largest source of resource references
#       outside ActivityKind, pulling in Material3 and androidx.cardview for
#       colours Tracks never renders. Shimmed in src/compat at ~30 lines.
#
#
#   service/devices/garmin/GarminSupport.java
#       The orchestrator, and the most entangled file in the set — it reaches
#       into GBApplication, the DAO session, preferences, and the UI. Our
#       equivalent lives in com.tracks.device.garmin and drives the vendored
#       protocol directly.
#
#   service/devices/garmin/communicator/
#       Pulled in a later pass with the BLE layer; it depends on GB's entity
#       classes for the download-then-import path we are replacing.
#
#   activities/, adapter/, widget/
#       Gadgetbridge's user interface. Tracks has its own.
#
#   entities/, database/
#       greenDAO. Tracks stores activity data on the server and mirrors
#       summaries in SQLDelight; a second ORM would be a second source of truth.
